state race; Blakeman has offered few policy details"], "rationale": "Quinnipiac and Siena show Hochul up nine to nineteen points among likely voters, raters unanimously place the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and markets price her win odds at 96 percent. The state's Democratic lean and her incumbency outweigh Blakeman's tax-cut messaging and Trump's planned surrogate appearances.", "watch": "If Republican-sponsored surveys showing a four-to-six-point race prove closer to the truth, or a late scandal or debate stumble hits Hochul, the contest could slip back toward Likely D.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Kathy Hochul (D) is running."]}, "overview": "New York's governor race pits incumbent Democrat Kathy Hochul against Republican Bruce Blakeman. The state's Democratic lean, Hochul's incumbency, and a steady polling lead frame the contest. September surveys from Quinnipiac and Siena College show Hochul ahead by nineteen and nine points among likely voters, and polls through the summer consistently put her up high single or double digits. Seven race raters place the contest in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and all three major prediction markets price her win probability at 96 percent.\n\nBlakeman has kept the race competitive on the airwaves, raising $13.4 million and pitching income tax cuts, a Staten Island bridge, and cost-of-living themes, with President Trump set to campaign alongside him. But he has offered few policy details, trails slightly in cash on hand, and faces an electorate tilted toward Hochul. The race sits firmly in the Democratic column, with cost-of-living sentiment the main avenue for a late shift.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9859, "margin": 12.77}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9584, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9776, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9779, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9761, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27"